solar-system-model-answer-why =
    In textbook pictures the planets are drawn side by side and big — or they would not show at all. In truth the Solar System is nearly empty: the Sun is a hundred times bigger than the Earth, and the Earth is a hundred of its diameters away. So in an honest model the Earth is a grain twenty metres from the ball, and Neptune half a kilometre off.

    And the planets move by an exact rule. The further a planet is from the Sun, the longer its orbit — and the slower it goes, as the Sun pulls it more weakly. Together that gives Kepler’s third law: T²/a³ is the same for all the planets. Kepler found it in 1619, sifting through observations; the law of gravitation explained it.
